# Lesson 5, new exercise: student opens 2NT with four spades.
title: Your 2NT, four spades
id: L05-23
type: exercise
lesson: 5
concepts: 5.7
student: S
board: 46
dealer: E
vulnerable: none

deal:            # spades  hearts  diamonds  clubs
  N  JT86   QT8    54     A643
  E  K73    752    T972   QJ2
  S  AQ54   AKJ    KJ63   K5
  W  92     9643   AQ8    T987

auction:
  E Pass
  S 2NT*   = 21-22 points, balanced
     key: 21 points and a balanced hand: open 2NT.
     not 1NT: 1NT shows 15-17. You have 21.
     not *: 21 points and balanced: open 2NT.
  W Pass
  N 3C     = Stayman: do you have a 4-card major?
  E Pass
  S 3S*    = Four spades, not four hearts
     key: Answer Stayman: 3♠ shows four spades.
     not 3D: 3♦ says "no major." You have four spades.
     not 3NT: Partner asked a question. Answer it.
  W Pass
  N 4S     = Four spades too: game
  E Pass
  S Pass
  W Pass

auction recap:
  You opened 2NT and answered Stayman with 3♠. Partner had four spades as
  well, so she bid the game. You are declarer, because you bid spades first,
  and so West will lead to the first trick.

planning:
  trumps out: 5
  remind: What is the contract? How many tricks do you need?

play:
  1   W CT  N C3  E C2  S CK
  2   S C5  W C7  N CA  E CJ
      note: Cross to dummy with the club ace, to lead trumps from there.
  3   N SJ  E S3  S S4*  W S2
      S4 key: Let the jack run. If East has the king, it can't escape.
      S4 not SA: Did you think about the finesse? If East has the king, the jack wins.
      note: A finesse: if East has the king, the jack wins.
  4   N ST  E S7  S S5  W S9
      note: Repeat the finesse.
  5   N C6  E CQ  S SQ  W C8
      note: Ruff a club to get back to your hand.
  6   S SA  W H3  N S6  E SK
      note: Draw the last trump. The king falls.
  7   S HA  W H4  N H8  E H2
      note: Trumps are drawn. Cash your top hearts.
  8   S HK  W H6  N HT  E H5
      note: And the king.
  9   S HJ  W H9  N HQ  E H7
      note: Over to dummy's queen of hearts.
  10  N C4  E D2  S D3  W C9
      note: Lead the last club. The defense is due some diamond tricks.
  11  W DA  N D4  E D7  S D6
  12  W DQ  N D5  E D9  S DK
  13  S DJ  W D8  N S8  E DT
      note: 11 tricks: 4♠ made with an overtrick.

play recap:
  The plan: cross to dummy so you can lead trumps toward your ace-queen.
  That finesse catches the ♠K if East has it. Then draw trumps and cash your
  hearts.

  4♠ made 11 tricks. 3NT would also have made, with nine, so the choice
  didn't cost the game this time. But 4♠ scored more, and with a 4-4 major
  fit the suit contract is usually the better bet.
